sec.t:
Life on Earth is far older than the Cambrian. For around 3 billion years, almost all living things were [[@first-life|microscopic]]. Then, a little before the Cambrian, strange soft creatures appeared, called the Ediacaran [[organism|organisms]]. Some looked like ribbed leaves, quilted mats or flat discs, and scientists still debate what many of them were.

In the Cambrian, things changed fast. Animals began making hard shells and skeletons. They burrowed into the sea floor, swam in open water and hunted each other. The burrows themselves are a clue. Rocks from before the Cambrian show mostly flat, simple trails on the surface of the mud. Cambrian rocks are churned with deep, twisting tunnels, a sign that animals had learned to dig for food and shelter.

{{story:eyes-see}}

That salt makes the water very dense. Your body is less dense than the salty water, so it pushes you up. Swimmers bob on the surface like corks and can lie back as if on a sofa. That push is called [[buoyancy|buoyancy]]. Crusts of white salt build up on rocks along the shore, sometimes in shapes like mushrooms.

It is not all fun. The water stings badly in eyes and cuts, and swallowing it can make you very ill. Lifeguards watch closely, and visitors are told never to dunk their heads.

{{story:floating-ships}}

Heatwaves often start when a large area of high pressure settles over a region and stays put. Air inside it sinks, warms and blocks clouds, so the sun beats down day after day. Meteorologists call it a heat dome. In June 2021, a heat dome over western Canada pushed the village of Lytton to 49.6 °C, the highest temperature ever measured in that country. A [[@forest-fires|wildfire]] swept through the village the next day.

Dry ground makes it worse. Normally, the Sun's energy is partly used to evaporate water from soil and plants, which cools them. When the ground is parched, more of that energy simply heats the air. In India, heatwaves usually strike from April to June, before [[@monsoon|the monsoon]] rains arrive. On the plains, the [[@imd|India Meteorological Department]] declares a heatwave when the temperature reaches at least 40 °C and is well above normal, or hits 45 °C whatever the normal. In May 2016, Phalodi in Rajasthan reached 51.0 °C, the highest temperature ever recorded in India.

{{story:sweat}}

In 1959, the American satellite Explorer 6 sent back the first crude picture of Earth from [[orbit|orbit]]. In 1966, a spacecraft circling the Moon called Lunar Orbiter 1 photographed Earth hanging over the Moon's grey edge.

The most famous version came on 24 December 1968. Three astronauts on [[@apollo-moon|Apollo 8]] were the first people ever to circle the Moon. As they came round its far side, Earth rose over the horizon, blue and white against black. Astronaut Bill Anders grabbed a camera loaded with colour film. His photo, called Earthrise, is one of the most printed pictures in history. Many people say it helped start the modern environmental movement. The first Earth Day was held just over a year later, in April 1970.

{{story:photography-history}}

Hornbills eat a lot of fruit, especially figs from trees such as the [[@banyan|banyan]]. They swallow fruit whole and later drop the seeds, often far from the parent tree. Some large forest trees depend on big birds like hornbills to carry their heavy seeds away.

Scientists studying hornbills in Arunachal Pradesh found that they move huge numbers of seeds every day. Because of this, people call them "farmers of the forest". Where hornbills disappear, those trees struggle to spread, and the whole forest slowly changes.

{{story:banyan}}

A weather balloon is made of thin rubber and filled with hydrogen or helium, gases lighter than air. It rises at about 5 m every second, roughly the speed of a lift in a tall building. In about two hours it reaches 30 km or more, nearly four times higher than Mount Everest.

As it climbs, the air around it gets thinner and colder, dropping below minus 50 °C. With less air pushing on the outside, the balloon stretches wider and wider until it finally bursts. A small orange [[@parachutes|parachute]] opens, and the radiosonde floats back down, sometimes landing in a field, a forest or the sea. In the United States, only about one in five is ever found and returned.

{{story:weather-forecasts}}

The Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations checks the health of fish stocks every two years. Its 2026 report found that about 62% of the stocks it tracks are fished at sustainable levels. The rest are being caught faster than they can breed.

The amount of fish caught from the wild has stayed at around 90 million tonnes a year since the late 1980s. All the growth since then has come from fish farms, known as aquaculture. In 2022, for the first time, farms produced more fish and seafood than wild fishing.

{{story:supply-demand}}

In the 1960s, the scientist Hans Kruuk spent years watching spotted hyenas in the Serengeti and Ngorongoro, often at night. He found that hyenas killed most of their own food. Lions, arriving later, often drove them off and ate it.

Hyenas can run at about 60 km/h and keep going for kilometres. They hunt in groups, and a clan can finish a zebra in under half an hour. Fights between hyenas and [[@lions-gir|lions]] over a kill are common, and the winner is usually whichever side has more animals there.

{{story:ethiopia}}

Tiny particles in the air can make sunsets even stronger. In 1883, the [[@volcano|volcano]] Krakatoa in Indonesia exploded. It threw fine dust and gas so high that the winds spread it around the planet. For months, people as far away as Europe and the United States saw blazing red skies. In some places fire brigades rushed out, thinking a distant town was burning.

Some art historians think the fiery sky in Edvard Munch's painting *The Scream* was inspired by those sunsets. Others think it simply shows the painter's feelings. Nobody can be sure.

{{story:mars-sky}}

When light is dim, your pupils, the black circles in the middle of your eyes, open wider to let more light in. Behind dark sunglasses, they do the same. If the lenses block light but not UV, the wide-open pupils actually let in more UV than before.

That is why dark toy sunglasses without a UV filter can be worse than no sunglasses at all. Good UV-blocking lenses can be any colour, even clear. Many ordinary spectacle lenses now include a UV filter too.

{{story:sunburn-sunscreen}}

In India, the olympiad programme is run by the Homi Bhabha Centre for Science Education in Mumbai, part of the Tata Institute of Fundamental Research. Students sit a qualifying exam, then a regional olympiad, then the Indian National Mathematical Olympiad. The best few dozen go to a training camp, and the final team of six is chosen there.

American students climb a similar ladder. It starts with the AMC contests, which tens of thousands take in schools, and passes through harder exams to the USA Mathematical Olympiad. The top students then join a summer training programme.

{{story:olympics}}

Why does it feel electric? Nerves really do send signals using [[@electricity-history|electricity]]. Tiny charged particles move in and out of nerve cells, passing a pulse along the cable like a wave in a stadium crowd. A sharp knock makes many nerve fibres fire all at once.

That flood of jumbled signals feels like an electric shock. It's similar to the fizz you get when a foot has "fallen asleep". In that case, pressure on a nerve stops signals for a while, and when it lifts, the nerve wakes up crackling with [[@body-pins-needles|pins and needles]].

{{story:body-pins-needles}}

In 1872, Charles Darwin wrote a book about how people and animals show emotions. He noticed that a frightened dog and a frightened person both widen their eyes, and he wondered whether expressions were inborn.

In the 1960s, American psychologist Paul Ekman travelled to Papua New Guinea to test this. He suggested six basic emotions found everywhere: [[@happiness|happiness]], sadness, fear, anger, surprise and disgust. Other scientists, such as Lisa Feldman Barrett, argue that emotions are more flexible. They say the brain builds each feeling from body signals and past experience, and that cultures label and show emotions differently. Both sides have evidence, and the debate is very much alive.

{{story:indian-classical-dance}}

Girls' bodies usually start changing first. Breasts begin to develop, hips widen and, a year or two later, periods begin. A period is when a small amount of blood leaves the body through the vagina, about once a month. It's part of a monthly cycle that prepares the body to be able to have a baby one day. Periods are a normal, healthy part of growing up, even though in many families, in India and elsewhere, people have felt shy talking about them.

Boys usually start a little later. Their shoulders broaden, muscles grow, and hair appears on the face. The voice box grows bigger, and its vocal cords get longer and thicker. For a while, the [[@voice|voice]] may crack or jump between high and low before it settles deeper. This is sometimes called the voice breaking, though nothing is actually broken.

{{story:singing}}

By then, Babbage was already thinking of something bigger. From 1834, he designed the Analytical Engine. It had a "store" to hold numbers, like a computer's memory, and a "mill" to do the arithmetic, like a processor.

Instructions would be fed in on punched cards, an idea borrowed from the [[@knitting-weaving|Jacquard loom]], which used cards to weave complex patterns into silk. The mathematician [[@ada-lovelace|Ada Lovelace]] wrote about the engine and described how it could be programmed. It would have been driven by steam, a true child of the [[@industrial-revolution|Industrial Revolution]].

{{story:industrial-revolution}}

Special relativity begins with one rule: light always travels at the same speed, whoever measures it. Einstein worked out that if this is true, then [[@future-time-travel|moving clocks must tick more slowly]]. The faster you go, the bigger the effect.

In 1915 he went further with general relativity. He said [[gravity|gravity]] is not a pull but a bending of space and time around heavy objects. Clocks run slightly faster high above the Earth, where gravity is weaker. That is why [[@gps-how|GPS]] [[@satellites-help|satellites]], which are both fast and high, must correct their clocks every day.

{{story:time-in-space}}

To work in messier places, robots must notice what is going on around them. [[@robots-home|A robot vacuum cleaner]] bumps into a sofa leg, feels the bump and turns away. Newer ones scan the room with cameras or [[@lasers|lasers]] and build a little map, so they can clean in neat rows.

The hardest part is understanding what the sensors see. A camera gives a robot millions of coloured dots. Working out that some of those dots are a child, a ball or a step is difficult. This is where [[@ai-what|artificial intelligence]] comes in: programs that learn to recognise things by studying huge numbers of examples. The same problem faces [[@self-driving|self-driving cars]], which are really robots on wheels.

{{story:mars-rovers}}

Victims usually developed fever and chills, then painful swellings called buboes in the neck, armpits or groin. This form is called bubonic plague. A more dangerous form infected the lungs and spread through coughing.

Nobody at the time knew about germs. Some people blamed bad air, the position of the planets or divine punishment. Doctors tried bloodletting, herbs and sweet-smelling flowers. In some towns, Jewish communities were falsely blamed for spreading the disease and were attacked, one of the darkest parts of the story.

{{story:antibiotics}}

Rome had public toilets called latrines. A typical one was a long stone bench with a row of keyhole-shaped holes, and no walls between seats. A channel of water ran underneath to wash waste into the sewers. Another small channel ran at people's feet.

Many historians think Romans cleaned themselves with a tersorium, a sea sponge on a stick, rinsed in that foot channel. Others think the sponge was for cleaning the toilets. The waste went into sewers, including the Cloaca Maxima, Rome's great drain, parts of which are still used today. That city's power and engineering are told in [[@ancient-rome|ancient Rome]].

{{story:space-toilet}}

Along the south bank lies Kaziranga National Park, home to about two-thirds of the world's greater one-horned rhinos. Each monsoon, floods cover much of the park. Animals move to higher ground, some crossing the national highway, where traffic is slowed to let them pass. The floods are hard on wildlife, but they also renew the tall grasslands that [[@rhino-kaziranga|rhinos]] feed on.

{{story:rhino-kaziranga}}

The fertile valley also grows much of India's tea, on estates that stretch over low hills on both sides of the river. At Sualkuchi, on the north bank near Guwahati, families weave golden muga silk, made by silkworms found almost only in Assam.

About 30 crore years ago, nearly all of Earth's land was joined in one [[@pangaea|supercontinent]] called Pangaea, meaning "all lands". Around 20 crore years ago it began splitting up.

The continents sit on huge slabs of rock called [[plate|plates]], which move a few centimetres a year, about as fast as your fingernails grow. Over millions of years, that is enough to open oceans. The Atlantic is still getting wider.

{{story:india-drift}}

People still live in the Sahara, often near oases, where underground water reaches the surface and date palms grow. The Tuareg, a people of the central Sahara, have crossed it for centuries with camel [[caravan|caravans]], trading salt, dates and cloth. Many Tuareg men wear a long blue or indigo veil wrapped around the head.

[[@camels|Camels]] suit the desert well. They can go days without drinking, then gulp down a huge amount of water in minutes. Their wide, padded feet stop them sinking in sand. Today trucks and four-wheel drives carry much of the trade, but camels are still used in many places.

{{story:camels}}

Yeast is a living single-celled [[fungus|fungus]], so tiny that a teaspoon of dried yeast holds billions of cells. It is related to the [[@mushrooms|mushrooms]] that pop up on lawns after rain. Bakers in [[@ancient-egypt|ancient Egypt]] were already making raised bread thousands of years ago, long before anyone knew what yeast was.

{{story:mushrooms}}

In India, naan and pav are raised with yeast. Dhokla batter is often lifted with fruit salt, a packet of soda mixed with citric acid that fizzes the moment it meets water.

The heat sensor that capsaicin triggers is called TRPV1. Scientists found it in 1997 by using capsaicin as a clue. They searched for the exact part of a nerve cell that reacted to chilli, and discovered that it also reacted to real heat.

The American scientist David Julius led that work, and in 2021 he shared the Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine for it. Understanding TRPV1 has helped doctors study [[@pain|pain]], and some pain-relief creams use capsaicin itself.

{{story:pain}}

Many festivals have a special sweet. At [[@ganesh-chaturthi|Ganesh Chaturthi]], families make modak, a steamed dumpling filled with coconut and [[@jaggery|jaggery]], said to be Ganesha's favourite. At Eid, families serve sheer khurma, made with vermicelli, milk and dates. At [[@christmas|Christmas]] in Goa, homes make kulkuls and [[@goan-food|bebinca]].

{{story:diwali-lights}}

At Diwali, boxes of sweets are exchanged between families, friends and neighbours. Temples prepare prasad, food offered to the gods and then shared. The laddu of the Tirupati temple in Andhra Pradesh is so famous it has a GI tag.
